From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 09:38:55 +0200
Subject: nv30: on the PS3, a SIFM copy's source has the room ps3rsx counts for
 it

ps3rsx refuses a scaled-image (SIFM) copy whose source might be read past the end of its buffer
(validate.c, sifm_check). It doesn't know whether the engine clamps its reads to the declared
IMAGE_IN_SIZE, so it counts the larger of that size and the texels the steps sample.
nv30_transfer_rect_sifm declares align(w, 2) x align(h, 2), so an odd-height source counts one row
more than it has. A texture upload into a swizzled texture copies the staging buffer by SIFM, and
the staging buffer held exactly the box's rows rounded to its pool class: when the class left less
than a row (a 33x21 RGBA box: 4032 bytes in a 4 KiB class, 4168 counted) the submission was
refused, and its draws and the upload were lost (SuperTuxKart, 15 s into its load).

- An odd-height upload's staging buffer gets one row more, after the layout: layer_stride and the
  offsets stay, and a stride (a multiple of 64) holds align(w, 2) texels.
- A linear texture gets one row more at its buffer's end, for its last level copied by SIFM
  (resource_copy_region into a swizzled texture, a resolve).
- nv30_transfer_sifm checks the kernel's count (nv30_sifm_extent.h, the kernel's formula on the
  words this file sends) against the source's buffer and leaves a copy that wouldn't fit to the
  next method: the 3D blit, or the CPU for a one-byte swizzled destination (mappable on the PS3:
  textures are made in the CPU-visible VRAM or in GART). A scaled copy never reaches the CPU path.
  The first such copy, and with NV30_PS3_IDX_STATS=1 every power of two, prints a line.

Nothing changes off the PS3. The kernel's rule stays as it is: the client now has the bytes it
declares.

Forwarded: no

